{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And Abraham, shall he become?</b> It is a midrashic aggadah: the memory of the righteous is for blessing, since Scripture mentions him in connection with blessing. And the plain sense of the verse, according to Rashi's language, is: would I conceal it from him? Is it not beloved before me that he become a great nation? (Rashi on Genesis 18:18). But the correct interpretation is that HaShem blessed be He spoke in honor of Abraham. He said: behold, he is destined to become a great and mighty nation, and his memory will be for blessing in his seed and among all the nations of the earth; therefore I will not conceal it from him, for future generations would say, How did He conceal it from him? Or, how was the righteous man cruel toward his neighbors who dwelt around him, neither showing mercy nor praying for them at all? And the revelation to him is good and proper, for I have known him — that he recognizes and knows that I am YHWH who loves righteousness and justice, meaning that I execute justice strictly with righteousness, and therefore he will command his sons and his household after him to keep my way. Now, if they are acquitted by the way of righteousness and justice, he will pray before me to spare them, and the thing is good; and if they are fully guilty, he too will desire their punishment. Therefore it is fitting that he enter the council of HaShem."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:31:17.545645978Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}